<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 setdef <kerrick@setdef.com>
SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT

The Windows launcher's application manifest, embedded in the program by
windows.rc, so it stays with the program when fledge build renames it.

longPathAware lets the process open files past MAX_PATH where Windows
allows it, as RubyInstaller's own ruby.exe declares; without it,
RubyGems fails to load from an app unpacked deep in a folder tree (the
Windows launcher spike). supportedOS names Windows 10 and 11, so Windows
runs the program without compatibility shims. requestedExecutionLevel
asInvoker runs it with the rights of whoever starts it, and turns off
Windows' installer detection, which would otherwise ask for an
administrator's password before running an app whose name contains
"setup", "install", or "update". activeCodePage UTF-8 makes UTF-8 the
process's ANSI code page (GetACP), from Windows 10 version 1903, so a
Windows function that takes a narrow string reads Ruby's UTF-8 paths as
UTF-8: ffi opens a gem's DLL with LoadLibraryExA, which under code page
1252 finds no DLL under a folder named outside ASCII. ruby.exe declares
no activeCodePage; doc/differences_from_ruby.md says what differs.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="asInvoker" uiAccess="false"/>
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings xmlns:ws2="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">
      <ws2:longPathAware>true</ws2:longPathAware>
      <activeCodePage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2019/WindowsSettings">UTF-8</activeCodePage>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
